t; its sub-clusters placed on the cluster map again at program-of-study depth; two jobs at different training levels compared on local and national figures: pay, training time and cost, hours, conditions, outlook); six field task sessions holding the cluster's four to seven sub-clusters, each its field task from the progression row (for example the help desk ticket queue, the card bracket tested to failure, the ramp to a slope rule, the barge loading plan) and its program of study for one job (high school courses, the training route, the first credential), each piece revised by a five-minute fix the next session (Sub-cluster checks); a cluster with seven sub-clusters runs its two shortest tasks in one session, and one with four or five gives its longest tasks two sessions; a plan day (one piece revised in full, the sub-cluster and job the student might narrow to, the plan's changed line with a figure, the goals reread, CD1.R3 carried); and the block assessment (mid-unit). **Own cluster packet** (days 31 to 41): eight packet days, self-guided, the same work on the course's task template, with check-ins on packet days 2, 5 and 8; then the unit's plan day, assessment day and respond day. Inside the blocks: practice 8 on the field task each block names (several ideas, two combined, tested against the field's constraint); practice 9 on every task with a budget, and one dated entry wage compared with the time and cost of training to the first credential; a field task that fails on the first try by design in each block, with where the student adapted (CD1.A4) and their steering habits rated (CD1.R4); a piece improved from feedback and the next step planned (ALL1.R2); which learning strategies worked on a field task, one changed (ALL1.R4); general work skills shown in each field task, logged with evidence for the portfolio (CD4.R3). Carried from grade 8 at higher demand: CTE.Career Clusters.Digital Technology; CTE.Career Clusters.Management & Entrepreneurship; CTE.Career Cl