ach every qualified applicant, defended with a dated who-holds figure, as a recruiter's work (CD2.A7), and the student's own plan to catch a stereotype at the next choice (CD2.R7). Carried from grade 8 at higher demand: CD3.K5; CD3.A5; CD3.K6; CD2.K7; CD2.K6; CD2.K8; CD5.K1; CD5.K2; CD5.K3; ALL2.K1; CD4.K5. Out: labor-market theory (grade 12 Economics); any verdict on a person or group | It comes after the three clusters because its judgments need the student's own job cards and data sheets (how open they have been, who holds the jobs they studied), and before the job pick because the pick should be made after the student has checked their own snap judgments and the forces outside them. It sits after the mid-year check, so the class has worked together for half a year before it reads pay and job gaps. Readiness items in the last ten minutes of check 1's respond day (course day 43): reading a dated who-holds figure as a pattern, never a verdict (g8-cd2-7-stereotypes, g8-cd3-6-uncommon-jobs, last used in Exploration unit 6, grade 8) and a ten-year outlook figure (g8-cd5-3-hiring-trends, Exploration unit 4). Retrieval: `alg1-percent-change-interest` (Algebra I, the year before) on the trends day. | Candidate: one unseen item reading a dated who-holds figure as a pattern; one unseen percent change computed from two dated projections. | Every section carries health's steps for when a student tells you something (the pay-gap unit, STANDARD.md section 6). Pay and job gaps by sex and race are shown with a dated source, never as a verdict on a person; a student never states their own group in class; first-generation, rural and tribal students and students who care for family or already work are planned for. Staying close to home is a real choice (family care, tribal and rural ties); no student is asked about family circumstances or moves. Luck and experience are answered from school, work or a public profile, never from family circumstances. The who-holds figures come from the federal labor statistics table of employment by occupation, sex, race and Hispanic or Latino ethnicity (https://www.bls.gov/cps/cpsaat11.htm); as of 2026-11-25 its 2025 annual averages are built from eleven months (October 2025 missing) and it gives sex as the percent who are women only, and the unit 3 data sheet says both. Tools: a who-holds-the-job figure reader and an outlook figure reader (shared screen, paper path); Curio Class writes the invented job ads, the downturn table extract with its source and year (the federal monthly jobs survey), the projection pairs, the regional figure sheets and the invented workers' accounts; real workers' public accounts are chosen with their source and date. Cost, class of 30, provisional: printing about 250 pages, about $13. Sensitivity read: women and men in jobs seldom held by their sex, Black, Latino, Asian American, Native and other workers in fields where they are few, disabled workers, and rural and tribal students who stay close to home (the agent first studies the federal labor statistics bureau's own notes on its employment-by-demographic tables, the federal women's bureau's pages, disability-led organizations' published employment pages and tribal nations' own published workforce pages); the health disclosure steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md). |
