# Career education: the standard, made usable

**DRAFT, 2026-11-16. Read cold 2026-11-20 (docs/reviews/prework-career-education-read-2026-11-20.md) and fixed;
second-model read and calibration findings fixed 2026-11-21 (docs/reviews/prework-career-education-fixes-2026-11-20.md).**

Written 2026-11-16 as the subject's pre-work (docs/PRODUCTION-PATH.md, "After the core subjects"; the owed row in
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md, "Before a scope and sequence in this subject"). The model is
docs/courses/social-studies/STANDARD.md. Two courses: Career Exploration (`cte-explore`, one year, typically grade 8) and
Career Pathways (`cte-pathways`, one year, typically grade 10), section 3. Personal finance is not a course: Economics teaches it, lightly
integrated (Cory, 2026-11-14), so it is not planned here.

## The owed row, and where it stands

| Owed | State | Where |
|---|---|---|
| Read the 2024 career-ready practices wording | Done 2026-11-16, from a state's reproduction (the publisher's page was not reachable); described in our own words | data/standards/cte-career-clusters-2024.txt header; section 1 |
| List what a school adds | Done | Section 4 |
| A learner standard for the two courses | Done 2026-11-21: terms read, 130 indicator ids loaded with our own words, each placed (data/plans/later.csv L91) | data/standards/ncdg-2024.txt and ncdg-2024-words.txt; sections 2 and 3 |
| A second state's rubric (the bar's "to firm it") | Owed | docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md part 7 |
| Progression gate support | Done 2026-11-21: scripts/progression.py splits ids on the tab, has `cte` in STD_FILES and ANCHOR and requires every learner-goal code through ELECTIVE_FILES; `progression.py check cte` passes (273 rows; 228 after the merge of the grade 10 learner-goal rows, 2026-11-21) | scripts/progression.py; data/progressions/cte.csv |
| Approach page | Approved by Cory 2026-11-21 (Desk q-prework-open-2) | docs/courses/career-education/APPROACH.md |

## 1. The standard and its terms

- **Which:** Advance CTE, Modernized National Career Clusters Framework, 2024 (guidebook June 2025). 14 Career
  Clusters (three cross-cutting: Digital Technology, Management and Entrepreneurship, Marketing and Sales), 72
  Sub-Clusters, and 12 Career Ready Practices, all in data/standards/cte-career-clusters-2024.txt.
- **The practices, 2024:** read 2026-11-16 in North Dakota CTE's copy of the 2024 list (dated 2025-06), because
  careertech.org's PDF is image-only and its practice page did not answer the reader. The twelve, in our words, are
  in the file header: professional contribution, clear communication, critical thinking and persistence, teamwork
  across differences, digital tools and data, resilience, managing time and space, creativity, stewardship of money
  and resources, planning one's own education and career path, environmental and social effects of decisions, and
  applying academic and technical skills. The 2024 list reframes the 2012 practices around the workplace and adds
  example tasks; numbering is by the 2024 order. To do when reachable: check the order against careertech.org itself.
- **Terms:** copyright Advance CTE, no reuse terms on the page (docs/reviews/standards-currency-2026-11-11.md). Cluster
  and practice names may be cited; the practice descriptions are never copied. Pages say "the national career
  clusters" in our own words and never claim alignment approved by anyone.

## 2. What the standard can and cannot do for these courses

The cluster framework says which fields exist and how they group; the practices say what a worker does. Neither says
what a grade 7 student should know or be able to do about planning a career. The bar (part 7) asks for a personal
plan, labor market data, career tasks (resume, interview, portfolio), hands-on work and defended choices.

**The learner goals.** Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-cs-career-library): the National Career
Development Guidelines (National Career Development Association, 2024 revision: three domains, goals and leveled
indicators) are the learner goals, with the clusters and practices as content. Fallback: if its terms bar ids and
our own words, Curio Class writes its own learning goals per course, marked "ours". The fallback is not needed: the
PDF (04/2024, read in text 2026-11-20 and 2026-11-21) has no copyright or reuse notice; NCDA's site legal notice
(read 2026-11-21) reserves all rights in site content and bars reproducing it without written permission, and its
framework page names writing lessons and tracking work by indicator code among the uses. So the ids are kept as
printed and every line about them is ours; no indicator sentence is copied (file headers). The guidelines have
three domains, eleven goals and 228 indicators coded like `CD1.K1`, in three stages (K, A, R) that are not tied to
an age. Its third domain is Social Emotional Learning (four goals, about a third
of the indicators), which the SEL courses already teach. So career education owns the Career Development and the
Academic and Lifelong Learning goals (seven goals), and the Social Emotional Learning goals are leans on the SEL
courses (Cory, 2026-11-21), so their 93 indicators are not in the ids file. The PDF is saved in
S:\CurioClass-sources\career-education\ncdg-2024\ (Cory's yes, 2026-11-21). The 135 Career Development and Academic
and Lifelong Learning indicators are in data/standards/ncdg-2024.txt (130 owned; 5 left out, section 3), with our own
line for each owned one in data/standards/ncdg-2024-words.txt.

## 3. How the standard maps to our courses (recommended, ours)

| Course | Clusters | Practices | Plan and tasks |
|---|---|---|---|
| Career Exploration, typically grade 8 | All 14 clusters surveyed, with a sub-cluster in each studied through real jobs and data | All 12 introduced, each practiced in a task | An interest and strengths inventory, a first education and career plan, a job study with labor market figures, a short interview practice |
| Career Pathways, typically grade 10 | Three clusters per student, studied at sub-cluster depth: two shared clusters the class chooses and one of the student's own through a self-guided packet, narrowing to one sub-cluster and one named job, with its program of study (courses, training, credentials) | All 12 practiced on field tasks | A study in depth of the student's chosen job, a full plan to its first credential, a resume, a mock interview, a portfolio, an applied project from the chosen job's field defended with data |

Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-cs-career-library): each course is one year (Career Exploration in
grade 7 or 8, Career Pathways in grade 9 or 10), at two or three sessions a week. The typical grades are 8 and 10
(see "Course list"); the frames say how many sessions.

**Pathways' width** (Cory, 2026-11-21: go broad, so students see more of what is open to them, even though it is
harder to keep current; Cory, 2026-11-22, Desk q-prog-cte-1: the class studies two clusters together and each
student adds one of their own). Pathways builds field tasks and a dated data sheet for all 14 clusters. Each student
meets three: two shared clusters the class chooses together, and one cluster of the student's own, any of the other
twelve, through a self-guided packet. Any of the 72 sub-clusters stays open through its cluster's field tasks, the
packet and the course's task template. Each data sheet carries its date and a line telling the teacher to check the
figures that year. Each training route on a job card is checked against the federal job outlook handbook's typical
entry level for that school year.

**Broad to narrow** (Cory, 2026-11-22, Desk q-prog-cte-1: students should be able to follow their interests from very
broad to narrow jobs; the path is ours, untested). Every student goes from a cluster, to a sub-cluster, to one named
job studied in depth. From any of their three clusters the student picks one sub-cluster, then one named job in it,
the job they choose, and studies its tasks, its training route and its pay and outlook figures (each with its source
and year), with one plan step toward it. The full plan (`g10-all2-full-plan`) narrows to that job's first credential,
and the applied project (`g10-prac-12-academic-skills`) takes its problem from that job's field. No job is the default,
and the plan is scored on its reasoning, never on the job chosen.

**Running it with one teacher** (ours, untested). The two shared clusters are taught whole-class: one room on one
field task with one data sheet on the same day. The own-cluster packet is self-guided: the cluster's dated data
sheet, its sub-clusters' field tasks on the course's task template, and the steps of the job study, each with its
model, so a packet stands alone for each of the 14 clusters. The frame sets packet days, when the whole class works
in its own packets at once, and check-in days within them, when the teacher meets each student briefly (the step
done, one figure, the next step, logged on the packet's check-in line). Skill days (resume, interview, the plan) and
the applied project are whole-class. On the 64-session short path the class studies one shared cluster instead of
two and keeps the own-cluster packet and the path to a named job. A frame may change the split with a reason.

**Course list** (settled by the expert reader, 2026-11-20). Two courses: `cte-explore` (grades 6 to 8 in the
catalogue, typical grade 8) and `cte-pathways` (grades 9 to 12, typical grade 10). The catalogue's typical grades are
set to 8 and 10 (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-career-1); a frame may place a course a year earlier with a reason
(section 7). Personal finance is
not a course (Economics, 2026-11-14). The progression file is data/progressions/cte.csv (subject code `cte`). The
catalogue's personal finance row and the five tool modules tagged to Exploration (three personal finance, two
economics) are retagged to Economics, and "personal finance" leaves the subject description (Cory, 2026-11-21);
that catalogue edit is a later step (data/plans/later.csv L90).

**Ownership** (settled by the expert reader, ours, untested). The progression has one row per owned item. For the gate,
a course's owner grade is its typical grade (Exploration 8, Pathways 10), as health and physical education use the
span's last grade; the frame may place a course a year earlier with a reason. Exploration owns the 14 clusters and 14 named
sub-clusters, one per cluster, fixed in the frame (Cory, 2026-11-21: a smaller number here, as long as breadth is
given somewhere). Pathways gives the breadth: it owns the other 58 sub-clusters, each met through its cluster's field
tasks and data sheet, so no sub-cluster is excluded (Cory, 2026-11-21). Owned here
means the course supplies each sub-cluster's teaching, task and check for the students who meet its cluster, shared
or through the packet; one student meets the sub-clusters of their three clusters ("Pathways' width"), and the course
page says so. The 12 practices
are owned by Exploration, where each is first taught in a task, and Pathways carries each as a `~` row with the field
task that practices it at higher demand. Placement rule for the learner goals (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-career-1),
the same rule as for the 12 practices: each 2024 National Career Development Guidelines indicator is owned in the course where it is first taught (Exploration, grade 8, or Pathways, grade 10), carried
in Pathways as a `~` row where Exploration owns it, and an indicator written only for adults already at work is left
out with a reason (section 5).

**Units in Exploration** (settled by the expert reader, 2026-11-21, ours, untested). The 14 clusters are grouped into
five cluster units of two to four clusters each, so each unit's closing days (APPROACH.md) are spent once per group,
not once per cluster: cross-cutting (Digital Technology, Management and Entrepreneurship, Marketing and Sales); making
and building (Advanced Manufacturing, Construction, Supply Chain and Transportation); land and energy (Agriculture,
Energy and Natural Resources); serving people (Education, Healthcare and Human Services, Hospitality, Events and
Tourism, Public Service and Safety); money and creative work (Financial Services, Arts, Entertainment and Design). A
frame may regroup with a reason. Each cluster row's study ends with a plan line the cluster changed; the unit's plan
day holds them.

**Learner goal placement** (ours, untested, 2026-11-21; ids in data/standards/ncdg-2024.txt). Of the 135 indicators,
Exploration owns 66, Pathways owns 64 and 5 are left out. The pattern: knowing (K) in Exploration; doing (A) in
Exploration where its tasks do it (the first plan, the job study with dated figures, the short interview practice),
else Pathways; judging (R) in Pathways, except re-examining goals, which Exploration's plan days do.

- **Exploration owns (66):** CD1.K1, A1, K2, A2, K3, R3, K4, K5, A5; CD2.K1, A1, K2, A2, K3, A3, K4, A4, K5, K6, K7,
  K8, K9, A9; CD3.K1, A1, K2, A2, K3, A3, K4, A4, K5, A5, K6; CD4.K1, K2, K3, A3, K4, K5, K6; CD5.K1, K2, K3;
  ALL1.K1, K2, A2, K3, A3, K4, A4, K5, A5, K6, A6, K7, K8; ALL2.K1, K2, K3, A3, K4, A4, K5, K6, K7.
- **Pathways owns (64):** CD1.R1, R2, A3, A4, R4, R5; CD2.R1, R2, R3, R4, A5, R5, A6, R6, A7, R7, A8, R8, R9;
  CD3.R1, R2, R3, R4, R5, A6; CD4.A1, R1, A2, R2, R3, A4, R4, A5, R5, A6, R6, K7, A7; CD5.A1, A2, A3; ALL1.A1, R1,
  R2, R3, R4, R5, R6, A7, R7, A8, R8; ALL2.A1, R1, A2, R2, R3, R4, A5, R5, A6, R6, A7, R7.
- **Carried:** every one of the 66 Exploration indicators is a `~` code in Pathways, on the Pathways row of the same
  goal (one row per goal per grade, with owned and `~` codes together), with the field task or plan step that uses it
  at higher demand; the handoff notes say grade 9 is skipped (as for the practices).
- **Left out, adult-only (5):** CD3.R6 (it judges what holding a job uncommon for one's group has done to a career,
  so it needs someone employed in one); CD4.R7 (it judges how a person has presented themselves inside a workplace
  they already work in); CD5.R1, CD5.R2, CD5.R3 (each judges the results of one's own career management over years
  against social, economic or hiring change, which a student does not yet have). Their A-stage partners stay in
  Pathways as plan work.
- **Notes:** CD2.A6 (experience shaping a choice) is answered from school or work, or for an invented worker, never
  from family circumstances (section 6). ALL1.K4, A4 and R4 name "learning style"; our lines teach the strategies
  and conditions that help a student learn, with no fixed learning-style label.

**Handoffs** (ours, untested). Each Pathways `~` practice row assumes Exploration's row for that practice, each
Pathways sub-cluster row assumes its cluster's row in Exploration, and each Pathways learner-goal row assumes the
Exploration row of the same goal, with the matching `hands_on` at the other end. The
handoff skips grade 9 because no career education course owns there; each such row's notes say so.

**The gate.** scripts/progression.py checks this subject: ids are split on the tab only, the cluster file is the
anchor, and every code in ncdg-2024.txt must be owned by a row.

Data the courses use: the US Bureau of Labor Statistics Occupational Outlook Handbook and wage data (public domain,
credit asked) and the O*NET database (CC BY 4.0, with its required attribution in the credit line only). See the scout.

## 4. What a school adds (quality that lies outside materials)

The course page says this plainly. A school adds:
- **People:** guest speakers, workplace visits, job shadows, internships or other work-based learning, and a counselor
  for one-to-one planning.
- **Its own context:** its state's programs of study, local labor market figures, its state's required plan (many
  states mandate an individual career and academic plan, often in a state system), dual-credit and credential offers.
- **Equipment and safety:** any field lab (tools, kitchens, shops, clinical kits) and the safety training it needs.
  Curio Class supplies simulations and paper tasks, not equipment.
- **Student organizations** for the field, if it has them.
- **Time:** a period or advisory slot the course runs in.

## 5. What is left out, and why

- Technical courses inside one cluster (a welding or nursing course): not on the course list, and the bar's part 7
  notes the framework "would not fit a technical one".
- Personal finance (Economics, 2026-11-14). Economics comes later (grade 12), so practice 9 is taught here as looking
  after an employer's money and materials and comparing a wage with costs, with the grade 7 math leans in section 7,
  and leans on no Economics row.
- The older 16-cluster framework (superseded; noted in the file).
- Learner-goal indicators written only for adults already at work: CD3.R6, CD4.R7, CD5.R1, CD5.R2, CD5.R3, each with
  its reason (section 3, "Learner goal placement"). The guidelines' Social Emotional Learning goals are leans, not
  owned (section 2).
- What a school or family may leave out (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-career-1), as for SEL, physical education
  and the arts: a unit on a sensitive sub-cluster (Military and National Security, Behavioral and Mental Health,
  Emergency Response, Judicial Systems, Public Safety, Resource Extraction). Every cluster has other sub-clusters, so
  a swap to one of the same cluster is offered; leaving out a unit means the course page and the unit's family note
  name its ids as not claimed that year.

## 6. Sensitive ground

No task asks a student about their own family income, immigration or work status, a criminal record or their health.
Job models use invented workers drawn from many groups, and a student's own resume uses a school address or none.

- **Pay and background:** pay and job gaps by sex and race are shown with a dated source, never as a verdict on a
  person; real workers from many groups counter stereotypes; first-generation, rural and tribal students, and students
  who care for family or already work, are planned for.
- **Disability at work:** disabled workers shown doing the work, with workplace rights stated plainly.
- **Paths:** no route is the default (apprenticeship, a certificate, two-year or four-year study, starting work, the
  military). The military is one real route with its costs and returns, neither promoted nor condemned, with no
  recruiter material on a student page; a teacher-page note says the school's own policy governs recruiter visits
  (Cory, 2026-11-21).
- **Clusters that touch other subjects:** Behavioral and Mental Health (health's ground), Emergency Response and
  Judicial Systems and Public Safety (violence, force, trauma), Resource Extraction (a live public dispute),
  Entrepreneurship and Regulation (political). Financial Services is taught as a cluster like any other (its jobs,
  training and tasks); only personal finance is Economics'. Environmental Protection is a needed field, not sensitive
  ground, and is taught like any other (Cory, 2026-11-22, Desk q-prog-cte-1).
- **Views on environment and resources** (Cory, 2026-11-22, Desk q-prog-cte-1): students' own views on environmental
  and resource questions are respected; no student is put down or graded for their personal views; tasks judge
  reasoning and evidence, not which side a student takes. The Environmental Protection and Resource Extraction rows
  apply it.
- **If a student tells you something** (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-career-1): every section on Behavioral and
  Mental Health, Emergency Response, Judicial Systems or Public Safety, and every section of the pay-gap unit (the
  unit that holds g8-cd3-6-uncommon-jobs, with g8-cd2-7-stereotypes and g8-cd3-5-open-jobs; in Pathways
  g10-cd3-a6-uncommon-in-plan), carries
  health's steps on its teacher page (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"), as SEL,
  physical education and the arts do.
- **The family note's question** (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-career-1): the question about a person's own work may
  be asked of a family member or instead of any trusted adult, answered for an invented worker, or answered from a
  public interview or profile, so no student is made to reveal a family's work status (APPROACH.md, unit field 9).
- **Interview and resume tasks:** no question an employer may not lawfully ask, and no mock interview that asks a
  student something private.
- **Hands-on tasks:** on paper or a simulation unless the school adds the lab; the unit's tools line names the safety
  step.

**Reads.** Every unit gets the fresh-agent sensitivity read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md, kind
`sensitivity`). Units on the Military and National Security, Behavioral and Mental Health, Judicial Systems, Emergency
Response, Public Safety and Resource Extraction sub-clusters, the pay-gap unit and any unit
that shows disabled workers, name that community or topic in the read's community slot.

## 7. Cross-subject leans

Exploration is placed in grade 8 for the gate, so it may lean on grade 7 and 8 core rows; Pathways is placed in grade
10 and may lean on grade 9 and 10 rows. A frame that places Exploration in grade 7 or Pathways in grade 9 swaps
each later-grade lean for its row of that grade (`e7-informative-writing`, `e7-revise-purpose-audience`,
`e7-present-salient`, `e7-collab-discussion`; `e9-w-informative`, `e9-sl-presentation`) and treats the grade 7 math
rows as taught alongside, not before. A resume or a cover letter is informative writing aimed at a reader, not one
of the writing standards' own text types.

- **ELA:** `e8-informative-writing` and `e8-revise-purpose-audience` (resume lines, an email to an employer),
  `e8-present-reasoning` (an interview answer), `e8-collab-discussion`, `e10-w-informative`, `e10-sl-presentation` (a
  portfolio defense).
- **Math:** `m7-percent-problems` and `m7-unit-rate-fractions` (wages, a budget), `m7-compare-populations` (reading a
  data display); `alg1-data-displays`, `alg1-percent-change-interest` and `alg1-correlation-causation` (Pathways),
  and the grade 7 rows above as prerequisites.
- **Social studies:** `ss8-public-budget` (reading public budget and price data). No grade 8 or grade 10 social studies
  row teaches labor markets (that is grade 12 Economics), so the course teaches what it needs of them from its own
  dated data sheets.
- **Computer science:** for the Digital Technology cluster, `g7-viz-choices`, `g8-relationships` and
  `g8-investigation-summary` (Exploration); `g9-data-dictionary`, `g10-visual-data`, `g9-optimize-procedures`,
  `g10-breach-causes` and `g10-career-skills` (Pathways).
- **Library:** judging a source by lateral reading: `library:g6-lateral-reading` (the method) and
  `library:g8-news-lateral` (Exploration), with `library:g8-research-product` (running a research cycle, for finding information);
  `library:g9-lateral-method`, `library:g9-source-log`, `library:g10-lateral-machine-text`, `library:g10-synthesis-map`
  and `library:g10-database-deep` (Pathways).
- **SEL:** self-knowledge, goals, teamwork and decisions: `sel:g8-sa-what-interests-me`, `g8-sa-my-strengths`,
  `g8-sa-who-i-am`, `g8-sa-i-can-try`, `g8-sa-skills-grow`, `g8-sa-snap-judgments`, `g8-sm-set-goal`,
  `g8-sm-plan-and-tidy`, `g8-sm-stay-on-task`, `g8-rel-teamwork`, `g8-rds-careful-thinking` and
  `g8-rds-who-is-affected` (Exploration); `g10-sa-what-interests-me`, `g10-sa-i-can-try`, `g10-sa-skills-grow`,
  `g10-sa-snap-judgments`, `g10-sm-set-goal`, `g10-sm-plan-and-tidy`, `g10-sm-stay-on-task`, `g10-rel-teamwork`,
  `g10-rel-different-ways`, `g10-rds-careful-thinking` and `g10-rds-who-is-affected` (Pathways).

Every lean in data/progressions/cte.csv was checked 2026-11-21 against its owner grade (52 distinct rows, each taught
at or before the leaning grade; docs/reviews/progression-career-education-2026-11-21.md).
# Career education: approach (layer 3)

**Approved by Cory 2026-11-21 (Desk q-prework-open-2). Written 2026-11-16, read cold 2026-11-20 (docs/reviews/prework-career-education-read-2026-11-20.md) and fixed; second-model read fixed 2026-11-21 (docs/reviews/prework-career-education-fixes-2026-11-20.md).** Short by design: it fills the subject
slots of the unit and section plan (docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md) and leans on the shared approach (docs/APPROACH.md),
the lens (docs/agents/lenses/career-education.md) and the standard (STANDARD.md). Ours, untested.

## The kinds of day, in career education's words

| Shared kind | In career education | What it looks like |
|---|---|---|
| Understanding | Explore day | A field, a job or a part of the self studied from a source: a job profile with dated data, a worker's account, an inventory used as reflection |
| Understanding on first teaching, Fluency after | Skill day | A career task taught with a model and practiced with feedback (its first teaching is Understanding, docs/GLOSSARY.md "teaching day"): a resume section, an interview answer, an email to an employer, reading a pay table |
| Application | Field task day or plan day | A task a field does, decided and defended with data; or the personal plan revised with what the unit found |
| Check | Assessment day | The end-of-unit assessment: a demonstration or plan piece scored on a rubric, plus a few short items on reading data |
| Respond | Respond day | Revision of the piece from rubric feedback |

Every unit has at least one field task day. Order of a unit's last days: field task day (the piece made, and the
plan's changed line drafted), plan day (written feedback on both returned, both revised), assessment day (scores the
revised piece and the plan, with a few short data items), respond day. A piece is scored only after that feedback and
revision (docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md, field 5). In Pathways the task is the same shape at higher
demand: a real data comparison, a defended decision, and a revision of the plan from feedback. Each practice taught in
a unit is judged with two or three look-fors on the piece the student produces, never on a list. Any task with
equipment follows STANDARD.md section 6 ("Hands-on tasks").

## The form of daily evidence

- **Explore days:** a short job or field card filled from the source, with the figure's source and year.
- **Skill days:** the practiced piece (one resume line, one answer), marked against two or three look-fors.
- **Field task and plan days:** the decision and one line of data that supports it; the plan's changed line.
- The portfolio holds the unit's best piece; students choose and say why (self-judging, bar C8).

## What career education adds to the plan fields

- Unit field 1: sessions by kind, sessions a week and minutes as the frame states them (see "Pacing").
- Unit field 2: the clusters and sub-clusters studied, the practices taught and the learner-goal ids (data/standards/ncdg-2024.txt),
  each with what the student does in our own words (STANDARD.md section 1); the data sources with their year; what
  is carried across the year (the plan and the portfolio) and the day a model is withdrawn.
- Unit field 3 (where it sits): builds on (the earlier unit's plan line), with the handoff marked strong or weak; leads to; comes back (the plan and
  portfolio, every unit); and the one link to this grade's core courses a teacher may use, by row id (STANDARD.md
  section 7).
- Unit field 4 (readiness): one short item on reading a table or a percent, and one on reading a dated figure; the
  ten-minute reteach is a model of the same reading.
- Unit field 5 (end-of-unit assessment): a demonstration, plan piece or portfolio piece scored on a rubric with two
  sample pieces at different levels, plus a few data-reading items. The plan is scored on its reasoning (a choice
  defended with a figure), never on which path the student chose or on the student's own circumstances. The scored
  piece had written feedback and a revision first (see "The kinds of day"). One line on accommodations: read aloud,
  large print, a scribe, an oral answer.
- Unit field 6 (error register): a national figure read as local; highest pay read as best; one training route (a
  four-year degree) read as the default; a job read as for one group of people.
- Unit field 7 (response allocation): the marking time and the response time, named separately, with the response on
  the respond day, counted once. The short data items are marked with the shared marking step (docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md,
  field 7). The rubric piece is judged by the teacher after class against two or three look-fors, never by hands-up
  self-marking. Respond-day groups come from the rubric cells marked "not yet" and the missed data items, at most four.
- Unit field 8 (tools and supplies): see "What a tool may and may not do"; every tool day has a paper path; any task
  with equipment names its safety step (STANDARD.md section 6).
- Unit field 9 (family note): in English and Spanish: what the unit explores, what a student may take home (the job
  card, the plan page), one question about a person's own work, to ask a family member or instead any trusted adult,
  or to answer for an invented worker or from a public interview or profile, so no student is made to reveal a
  family's work status (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-career-1); a line that sharing is the student's choice; and,
  for a unit the school leaves out, its ids not claimed that year (STANDARD.md section 5).
- Unit field 10 (sources): a sources table (read status, what is from memory), the data sources with their year, and
  a line that no study stands behind the course and that the logic model is on the course page (C16).
- Section field 1: the id of the cluster, sub-cluster, practice or learner goal, the share of it the section carries,
  what the student does in our own words (cluster ids are names, not verbs, and the practice text is never quoted,
  STANDARD.md section 1), and the data source with its year. The teacher's note on the field (what the work is,
  training routes, conditions) goes in the section's before-you-teach text, with any note STANDARD.md section 6 asks
  for on a cluster it names. A section on Behavioral and Mental Health, Emergency Response, Judicial Systems or Public
  Safety, or in the pay-gap unit, carries health's steps on its teacher page (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a
  student tells you something"; Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-career-1).
- Section field 2 (where it sits): what comes just before and just after; the readiness items it uses, and where its
  own results go.
- Section field 3: the numbers of the end-of-unit assessment items this section owns, then each day's evidence in the
  form above.
- Section field 4 (expected errors): each letter as a wrong example beside a right one (a pay figure with no year; a
  plan line with no reason), the day it is shown, and the five-to-ten-minute action if many made it.
- Section field 5: what is carried: the student's plan and portfolio, returned to each unit; the model piece, held by
  the teacher on the skill day and withdrawn on the field task day.
- Section field 6: kind of day; how a field task day opens (the decision to make and the data sheet).
- Section field 7 (talk and writing): the talk routine for a job comparison (claim, figure, source); one written
  task; interview practice with the teacher or a card as the interviewer, a model answer first.
- Section field 8 (practice that returns): one earlier skill (reading a pay table, one resume line) tried again on a
  new field.
- Section field 9 (supports and extension): English learners: six to eight workplace words in depth with a picture,
  a sentence frame for each interview or comparison step, and a key line that separates a language gap from a content
  gap. Access: any spoken task can be done in writing, with a card or with the teacher. Reading load: a job profile is
  at most 150 words at the course's reading level; the rest is read aloud. Check accommodations: read aloud, oral
  answer, scribe, extra time. Extension: the same task on a less familiar field, never the next course's standard.
- Section field 10 (two rooms): a parent teaching one child at the table (a paper job card, a family member as the
  interview practice partner); one projector (one profile shown and compared by volunteers).
- Section field 11 (not in this section): what a school adds (speakers, visits, a counselor, a lab), with the paper
  path when a school cannot.

## What a tool may and may not do

A tool may give a dated job profile, practice reading a pay table or an outlook figure, a private plan template, and a
mock-interview question bank with a model answer and a feedback list. It may not store a student's plan, interests or
answers off the device, score a person, or tell a student what job to do.

## Sensitive ground

See STANDARD.md section 6: what no task asks, the paths, the clusters that touch other subjects, the steps when a
student tells you something, the family note's question, and the reads. What a school or family may leave out, with
the same-cluster swap, is STANDARD.md section 5.

## Pacing

Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory): each course is one year at two or three sessions a week. Default time (settled by the expert
reader, ours and untested): the 32-week year of docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, three sessions a week of 45 minutes, so 96
sessions or 72 hours a year; a school at two sessions a week runs the short path, 64 sessions or 48 hours. The scope
and sequence names the 32 sessions the short path drops; every unit keeps a field task day, its plan day, its
assessment day and its respond day. No national body publishes a time figure for these courses that we found (Advance CTE, the Association for
Career and Technical Education and NCDA searched 2026-11-20; the guidelines' stages are not tied to an age), so none is
cited, and the course page says so. Each frame may change these numbers with a reason.

## Evidence and sources

Read: the 2024 cluster framework and practices (a state's reproduction of the practices; the publisher's own page did
not answer); the 2024 career development guidelines in text (2026-11-20 and 2026-11-21; NCDA's terms read 2026-11-21, STANDARD.md section 2); the base-text scout's
data sources (public domain labor statistics, CC BY occupational data). No claim of effect is made for any course.

## Questions in this draft

- Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-cs-career-library): the 2024 national career development guidelines
  as learner goals, our own as the fallback; each course one year (Exploration grade 7 or 8, Pathways 9 or 10), two
  or three sessions a week (STANDARD.md sections 2 and 3). Typical grades 8 and 10 (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk
  q-fix-career-1).

