# Curio Class: the foundation (layer 1)

Gathered 2026-11-11 (Cory). This is the one home for what Curio Class is for, who it serves, what it promises and
what it assumes. Every layer above it (the approach to teaching, the progressions, the scopes and sequences, the section plans, the
tools) is built on this page and may not contradict it. Until today these lines were spread over docs/VISION.md,
docs/ROADMAP.md, docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md and docs/agents/decisions/BASELINE.md; those files keep their detail and now
point here. Where one of them disagrees with this page, this page wins and the other file is fixed.

It changes rarely, and only with Cory. The layers and the order of work are in docs/ROADMAP.md ("The layers").

## 1. What it is for

A free, complete, open K-12 classroom toolkit: interactive tools, printables, lessons and units for every standard,
so a teacher can run a full course without paying for curriculum or software (VISION, Mission).

**The end goal.** A teacher can run any course on Curio Class from the first day to the last, free, with no account,
and the course would stand up to a state adoption review. A course is a grade of a subject or a high school course.
Every core subject is planned top down (progression, course frames, scopes and sequences, unit plans) before days
are written (docs/PRODUCTION-PATH.md, Cory 2026-11-13); each course is finished as a whole before it is called
finished. The earlier pilot-first bend (2026-11-11) is retired; the probe and guard replace it.

**We own the full course** (Cory, 2026-11-11; base texts added 2026-11-12). Curio Class owns every course: its
shape, tone, format, gates and reads. Where a well-reviewed open program carries a licence as open as ours (CC BY
or public domain), it can be the course's **base text**: adapted into Curio Class days, molded to our needs and
voice, with a credit line that says it was changed (math: Illustrative Mathematics). Programs under non-commercial
or share-alike terms are evidence only, for order, time, models and anchors, never their wording or problems,
so every page stays CC BY 4.0. We do not stitch two programs together: each hangs together through its own routines and pictures, and a
mix is weaker than either. A tool built to a clear job in our unit will also fit the same lesson in a course a
teacher already uses, and that is welcome, but it is not the plan.

## 2. Who it is for

| Who | What that means for the work |
|---|---|
| **A classroom teacher** running a course | The main reader of every scope and sequence, section plan and teacher page. Teacher pages teach the teacher |
| **A parent teaching one child at home** (named 2026-11-11; how far, decided 2026-11-12) | The second audience, served by the same course, never a separate track. Every lesson day carries an "at home" block (a 30 to 40 minute one-child path, what the parent says where the class has partner talk, which items to do, home swaps for materials); a course gives placement from its readiness checks, a numbered calendar ("36-week" in Cory's 2026-11-12 call; the school calendar is 160 days over 32 weeks, docs/courses/CALENDAR.md) and a record that works as a portfolio; answer keys explain, not only list; sample pieces show what grade-level work looks like. Not planned: separate home courses or multi-age plans |
| **A student alone** | The site is public and a child may open any tool with no adult present. Every tool stands alone: defaults work for a solo user and teacher toggles are extras (BASELINE rule 4) |

## 3. The promises

These hold even where a review, a district system or a convenience would reward breaking them.

1. **Free for good.** No paywall, ever (ROADMAP, Goal).
2. **No accounts, no ads, no tracking, no student analytics** (VISION, "What we will not do"). A tool cannot
   remember a student from one day to the next and there is no live teacher dashboard. We chose that.
3. **Results live with the student and the teacher.** A score code the student shows, and printable record sheets.
   The score code records only what a tool can mark (ROADMAP, "What a finished course holds", item 4).
4. **Every tool works offline as a single file.** Where a tool stores anything, it says on screen that it never
   leaves the device (BASELINE rule 7).
5. **Paper is a first-class product.** Worksheets, units and printables matter as much as screens. Printables read in
   black and white.
6. **Honest words.** We say taught, practiced or shown, per the ledger, never more. Practice tools are not
   diagnoses and score codes are not proof. We never say Curio Class was reviewed by, meets or is endorsed by
   anyone. Public copy names no companies (BASELINE rule 15).
7. **Classroom-safe, and plain about hard facts.** History and science are not softened; they are told with care
   for the people involved, and a fresh agent checks sensitive content before it goes live (BASELINE rules 5, 6, 8).
8. **We do not copy a branded product** in name, look or text, and we respect every licence: read and link, adapt
   only where the licence allows.

## 4. The bar

State adoption is the bar we build to (Cory, 2026-11-10; BASELINE rule 20). A full course should meet the substance
of what state and national curriculum reviews ask for. We do not pursue formal adoption now. The bar applies to
courses and the shelves that feed them; Curiosities and Recess are outside it. The bar never outranks the promises
above. What it asks and what we took from it: docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md. The rubrics we read ourselves against are filed
as docs/RUBRIC-*.md.

**Quality over speed, and over saved work** (BASELINE rules 1, 2, 14). Each piece is really good before the next
wave grows. An existing spec or tool carries no weight against what a unit needs: if the unit needs something
different, the old work loses.

## 5. What we assume about the room

Decided 2026-11-11 (Cory) unless marked.

1. **Grades 3 to 12: one device per student on a tool day.** The courses are designed that way and say so plainly.
   Every tool day still carries one line for a single projector, because some rooms have nothing else and a reviewer
   will ask.
2. **Kindergarten to grade 2: one shared classroom device**, always with a paper path (Cory, 2026-11-14).
3. **The physical world is written into the lesson plan.** A tool does not replace rolling the marble, using a
   protractor or reading a whole book. Hands-on work, materials and their cost are part of the unit, with a path for
   a teacher who has nothing (docs/reviews/science-4-spine-2026-11-11.md, finding 7).
4. **A teacher judges open work.** A tool marks what has a right answer. For writing, argument and discussion, the
   tool gives the work a structure so it is quick to read, and print carries the rest: a rubric, sample pieces at
   each score level, and a look-for sheet. Our job is to make that judging fast and consistent (BASELINE rule 21
   already says a tool cannot mark writing or talk).
5. **Time.** A scope and sequence states its own year and period length and gives a shorter path where ordinary schools have less
   time than a full program assumes.

## 6. What a tool is for

Decided 2026-11-11 (Cory), from the discussion recorded in docs/agents/decisions/DESK-ANSWERS.md.

1. **A tool is the end piece of instruction, a means to a defined end.** The end is defined in a section plan: what
   each day is for, where a tool is used and for what job. **No tool is built or refined until a section plan names
   its job.**
2. **A tool can be whatever the unit needs.** The section plan asks, for each key moment, what the ideal instrument for
   that idea would be. Sometimes that is a tool nobody has specced; sometimes it is a marble and a ramp. The limits
   are the promises and assumptions on this page and the cost of doing a tool well, not what software can do.
3. **Key tools and light tools.** A key tool is the one a day is planned around: it does the day's main job in a
   way print cannot (feedback on each try, help that fades as the student succeeds, many cases fast), and the paper
   path is the fallback (reworded 2026-11-11; it had read "one the unit cannot run without", which no tool can meet
   while every plan must also run on paper). Key tools are built first and hold the course up. Light tools (called
   practice tools before 2026-11-11) are lighter, built from shared templates over the long term, and still correct
   and still meet the three hooks. Lighter never means lower quality. A course is runnable when its key tools are
   live and print covers the rest. "Key" is a mark given by the section plan; the roles of BASELINE rule 21 (teach,
   see why, practice) still say what a tool does.
4. **Curiosities and Recess** are play and wonder. They claim no standard and are outside the bar (VISION).
5. **Tools and specs made before this page are sorted by demand** (Cory, 2026-11-11; BASELINE rule 26). Named by a
   section plan: course work. Live, passing the teaching test, not named: stays on the shelf, free-standing, and
   claims no course coverage. Everything else: frozen. A script makes the list; nobody argues a tool into a course.

## 7. How the work is checked

1. **The curriculum expert reads cold, often** (docs/agents/EXPERT.md). Cory's attention and a teacher's are the
   scarce things, so frequent cold reads by the expert, which teaches itself from outside sources first, are the
   main review at every layer. It is the main read, not the only one (changed 2026-11-11, when Cory accepted the cold strategic review). The
   writer and the expert are the same kind of model, so a cold read removes anchoring but not a shared mistake.
   Three things stand beside it: the sources the expert must open before it judges (the pedagogy and standards
   data, which Cory expects to grow), a script for everything that can be computed (item 2), a Codex read of a
   different model kind, and the agent reads in item 3.
2. **A machine checks what a machine can** (BASELINE rule 27). Standard codes, day counts, coverage of every part
   of a standard, and every answer that can be computed. A key the script cannot reproduce fails the build.
3. **Reads beside the expert** (Cory, 2026-11-11; BASELINE rule 28; changed 2026-11-14). Every row below is
   required before the course it touches is called finished. On 2026-11-14 Cory said there is no outside support
   ("We have no support. Our agents are our best hope for success"): the content rows are agent reads, each by a
   fresh agent that studies its sources first (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md). No outside human reader is
   planned. Pilots are retired (2026-11-13): the probe and guard (docs/pipeline/PROBE-GUARD.md) take their place.

   | Who | What they read or do | When |
   |---|---|---|
   | The expert agent (was a working teacher on the pilot unit) | Reads every plan and the probe and guard days against the subject lens and rubric | Every unit plan; the probe and the guard |
   | The answer-key agent (was a math content reviewer) | Solves every item of one whole course's checks cold and compares with the key, beside the script check | Before a math course is called finished |
   | The safety agent (was a science teacher) | Safety and materials of every investigation, against published school science safety guidance; and every PE unit with striking tools, mats, climbing, resistance training or an outdoor trip, against published school PE safety guidance (Cory, 2026-11-21) | Before a science course is called finished; before such a PE unit goes live |
   | The rights agent (was a second reader on rights) | The licence and source of every text a course teaches from | Before an ELA text is taught |
   | The Spanish agent (was a native Spanish reader) | Family notes, and student materials if they are made | Before Spanish is published in a course |
   | The sensitivity agent (was a health specialist) | The sensitive health topics, against public health guidance | Before those units go live |
   | The sensitivity agent (was a reader from the community concerned) | Native, cultural, disability and hard-history content, after studying the community's own published voice | Before those units go live |

   Rights and Spanish became agent reads on 2026-11-14 (DESK-ANSWERS, the continuity review's four questions); the
   funding rows for a paid Spanish review and teacher pilots stay, marked "only if funded".
4. **Lower layers are solid enough to build on, and expected to be revised** by what is built on them. No layer is
   declared finished in the abstract.
5. **What goes to Cory:** scope, audience, money, accounts, outside people, a live deploy, anything that moves a
   standard between sections, and anything this page cannot settle. Item-level choices inside the standards' limits
   are settled by the expert and reach Cory in a weekly digest (BASELINE, "What goes to Cory").

## Open questions on this page

- Closed 2026-11-14: the device assumption for kindergarten to grade 2 (section 5).
- Closed 2026-11-11: what happens to existing tools and specs that no section plan asks for (section 6, item 5).
- Closed 2026-11-24: "What a finished course holds" items. In: accessibility to a named level, a versioning and
  errata rule, a course teacher guide, a state alignment document. Spanish student materials after the core (L60).
  Not now: a print-volume count (docs/ROADMAP.md).
### Step 2. Course frames for the four core subjects, then a review: baseline 1

A course frame says what the course is: its grades, the standards and progression skills it owns, its days, its
unit list with a reason for each unit, and, where there is a base text, the source map (a section of the frame,
every base lesson placed in one of our units). The day-level data/plans/<course>/source-map.csv comes with the
scope and sequence, scripted from the source's free pages, then judged for unit edges and day counts; grade 4
math's table of 149 IM lessons is already scripted and kept outside the repo in the source store. A course frame is
the course-level summary; its scope and sequence (step 3) then sets every day in order. The 52 core courses of the
four subjects get one each (the 6 electives in these subjects wait, see After the core subjects). Scopes and
sequences already on file are read as input against the progressions, not assumed right.

Then **baseline 1**, a whole-structure review:
- every frame against its progression rows, and the frames of each subject read side by side through the grades;
- where the layers, the gates or the vocabulary need alignment or fixing;
- **the website design check**: whether the site's structure (catalog pages, topics, course and unit pages) still
  fits the hierarchy, and what changes if not. Proposals go to Cory; nothing is rebuilt during planning unless he
  says;
- a Codex read of a sample.

### Step 3. Social studies: scope and sequence for every course, then a review
### After the core subjects

Every later subject runs by docs/COURSE-RUN.md from stage 1 (pre-work) on. The order below still stands.

First the 6 electives of the four core subjects (precalculus, statistics, calculus, earth and space science,
environmental science, psychology). Then the 49 courses of the other subjects (47 active plus the 2 shelved world
language courses): arts and music, health and PE, computer science, career and technical education, social and
emotional learning, English language development, and library and research skills; world languages stay shelved
(settled 2026-11-13, see DESK-ANSWERS). Personal finance is not a course of its own: Economics teaches it, lightly
integrated, and data/courses.csv does not flag it core (settled 2026-11-14, see DESK-ANSWERS). Where a core
progression leans on one of the later subjects (English language development with ELA; health with science), the
cross_subject column notes it for then.

**Before a new subject's scopes** (lead 2026-11-15, see DESK-ANSWERS). Every elective and every later subject passes
these before Codex writes its first scope:
1. Its data layouts are gated before any writer runs: days.csv and source-map.csv in sequence-write.md's layout,
   checked by sequence.py, and the subject's progression checked. No writer invents a column.
2. One pilot course per subject first (Codex write, both reads, fix), then the brief is frozen for the rest of that
   subject; only a finding that breaks a gate reopens it.
3. The rule 2 readiness and retrieval count runs in the scope gate (sequence.py rule2_notes) on every course, so the
   gaps show at scope stage, not at the skeleton.
4. The unit skeleton runs on the pilot course before the others are written, so its adapter needs are known early.

### People and a second reader, through all of it


Tested and included above: Clark, Kirschner, Sweller (2012); Leahy et al. (2005); Nickow, Oreopoulos, Quan (2020); the CCSSM document itself; NYSED mathematics research briefs; ELSF ELA guidelines; two further WWC reading guides.

Not tested in this pass (flag only, test before relying on them): Hattie and Timperley, The Power of Feedback (2007); a cognitive load theory summary such as the NSW CESE paper; Clements and Sarama on learning trajectories; Fyfe et al. (2014) on concreteness fading; the EdReports v2.0 FAQ; the adoption rubric of whichever state Curio Class targets first.

## Evidence notes (added 2026-11-11 with the second draft of the approach)

The study detail that docs/APPROACH.md and docs/APPROACH-MATH.md leave out to stay short. In our own words. Page
checks are in docs/reviews/approach-2026-11-11.md.

- **Sinha and Kapur 2021.** A meta-analysis of 53 studies, 166 comparisons of problem solving then instruction
  against instruction then problem solving. Understanding and transfer: Hedges' g about 0.36 in favour of problem
  first. Procedures: no difference (about -0.03). Grades 2 to 5 (25 of the 166 comparisons): about -0.09, in favour
  of instruction first, and different from the other grades. Grades 6 to 10: about 0.50. General skills such as
  controlling variables: about -0.17. The effect was larger when the teaching built on students' own attempts (0.56
  against 0.20). The abstract's larger bias-adjusted figure (0.87) is not used. Most studies were math and science.
  Read in part in a browser; the discussion section was not read.
- **Hmelo-Silver, Duncan and Chinn 2007.** Abstract only: argues that problem-based and inquiry teaching are heavily
  supported, not unguided. No number or study may be attributed to it.
- **Pashler et al. 2007.** Levels: spacing moderate (1); worked examples with problems moderate (2), stated for
  math and science problem solving; graphics with words moderate (3); concrete and abstract connected moderate (4),
  with the warning that concrete-only learning may not transfer, and a mention of fading the concrete; pre-questions
  low (5a); quizzing strong (5b); deep explanatory questions strong (7). This guide's lowest level is called Low.
- **National Mathematics Advisory Panel 2008.** Finding 11: automatic recall needs enough practice. Finding 23:
  neither all student-centered nor all teacher-directed teaching is supported. Finding 25: regular formative
  assessment helps in the elementary grades. Finding 26: real-world problem teaching helped on similar problems
  only, from a small number of studies. Finding 27: explicit teaching has consistently positive effects for
  students with math difficulties. Finding 28: practice and tutorial software has generally shown positive effects;
  a large national study found none under typical conditions. (11, 25 and 27 were checked by the expert review.)
- **Nickow, Oreopoulos and Quan 2020.** 96 randomized trials of one-to-one or small-group tutoring; pooled effect
  0.37 standard deviations; 11 of the 96 were parent tutoring, with smaller effects. A working paper.
- **Bloom 1984.** The "two sigma" claim rests on two doctoral studies of about three weeks. Not repeated as a rule.
  The copy read is on an unofficial host.
- **Adams et al. 2008.** Interviews with college undergraduates using simulations, not a controlled trial.
- **Black and Wiliam 1998.** Used without an effect size on purpose; later re-analyses found smaller effects (the
  expert names Kingston and Nash 2011, from memory, not read).
- **WWC 2021, recommendation 6.** Timed activities of one to five minutes, as one part of taught small-group
  intervention, on material already taught, with feedback. Not a whole-class finding.
- **EEF maths report 2022.** Written for ages 7 to 14.

Cory, 2026-11-11: go forward without library access. The paywalled items below stay unread unless a free copy
turns up, and no page may rest on them.

**Not yet read, in order of value** (the first is the claim closest to what Curio Class builds):

1. A review of on-screen (virtual) manipulatives, such as Moyer-Packenham and Westenskow 2013.
2. Carbonneau, Marley and Selig 2013 (manipulatives meta-analysis) and Fyfe, McNeil, Son and Goldstone 2014
   (concreteness fading): the direct evidence for concrete, then pictures, then symbols.
3. Hattie and Timperley 2007 on feedback (the task and the next step, not the child).
4. The discussion section of Sinha and Kapur 2021; the full text of Hmelo-Silver et al. 2007 and Kirschner et al.
   2006 (library access).
5. Anything on parents teaching their own child a full course.
6. Learning trajectories (Clements and Sarama), when a K to 2 math course opens.

