g10-sub-financial-services-accounting | Field task day at higher demand than grade 8. From a quarter of an invented small business's records, the student builds a simple income statement, explains the largest change from the last quarter to the owner, and defends the note with the totals. The student also maps the program of study for one job, such as a bookkeeping clerk (certificate), an accountant (four-year study) and a certified public accountant (four-year study, more credits and a state license): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-financial-services-banking-credit | Field task day. The student checks an invented small business's loan application against a given lending checklist (income, costs, what secures the loan), writes the recommendation note, and defends it with the figures. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a bank teller (on-the-job training), a loan officer (four-year study) and a credit analyst (four-year study): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-financial-services-investments | Field task day. For an invented community group's reserve fund, the student compares two invented options by fees and a range of past yearly results, writes a recommendation that states the risk, and defends it with the figures. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as an investment operations clerk (on-the-job training), a financial analyst (four-year study and licensing exams) and a portfolio manager (experience): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-financial-services-insurance | Field task day. The student rates invented property cards with a given flood zone map and rating table, writes the reason for one rating, and defends it with the map and table. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a customer service representative (on-the-job training), a claims adjuster (a state license) and an actuary (four-year study and exams): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-financial-services-real-estate | Field task day. The student prices an invented house from three comparable sales using price per square foot, writes a listing that claims nothing the data does not support, and defends the price with the figures; the fair housing rule is stated plainly. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a leasing agent (on-the-job training), a real estate agent (a state license course) and an appraiser (a license with supervised hours): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-healthcare-human-physical-health | Field task day at higher demand than grade 8. The student plans an invented clinic's staffing for a day from patient volume by hour and each worker's scope of practice, and defends the staffing with the volume figures; the program of study for one of the jobs is mapped from high school courses to a first credential. The student also maps the program of study for one job, such as a nursing assistant (certificate and a state test), a respiratory therapist (two-year study and a license) and a physician (four-year study, medical school and residency): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-healthcare-human-behavioral-health | Field task day. The student routes invented intake cards at a community clinic to the right worker (a peer support specialist, a counselor, a social worker, a psychiatric nurse) by each worker's scope and training, reads dated workforce shortage figures by region, and defends one routing. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a peer support specialist (training and a state certificate), a mental health counselor (graduate study and a license) and a psychiatrist (medical school and residency): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-healthcare-human-biotech | Field task day. On paper the student plans a dilution series from a given stock, keeps a sample tracking table, catches one labeling error in a supplied log, and defends the protocol with the figures. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a lab assistant (certificate), a biological technician (two-year or four-year study) and a research scientist (doctorate): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-healthcare-human-social-services | Field task day. The student matches an invented household's case card to services in a given community directory by each service's eligibility rules, writes the referral note, and defends the match with the rules. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a social and human service assistant (high school and on-the-job training, sometimes two-year study), a case manager (four-year study) and a licensed social worker (graduate study and a license): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-healthcare-human-health-data | Field task day. The student codes invented clinic visit records from a short given code list, finds the errors in a supplied batch, reports the error rate, and states the privacy rule that protects patient records. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a medical records specialist (certificate), a health information technician (two-year study) and a health services manager (four-year study): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-healthcare-human-personal-care | Field task day. The student books an invented salon's day from service times and the sanitation step between clients, and reads the dated training hours each state asks for a license, defending the booking with the times. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a shampoo assistant (on-the-job training), a barber or cosmetologist (program hours and a state license) and a salon owner (license and experience): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-hospitality-culinary | Field task day at higher demand than grade 8. The student costs a menu for one hundred guests, writes a prep timeline and allergen labels, and defends one swap that keeps the cost per plate under the limit. The student also maps the program of study for one job, such as a line cook (on-the-job training), a chef (culinary certificate or two-year study and experience) and a food service manager (two-year or four-year study): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-hospitality-accommodations | Field task day. From an invented small hotel's booking table and housekeeping time per room, the student sets room assignments and the number of housekeepers needed, and defends the plan with the figures. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a front desk clerk (on-the-job training), a housekeeping supervisor (experience) and a lodging manager (two-year or four-year study): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-hospitality-events | Field task day. The student plans an invented school banquet: a room layout, a budget per guest and a timeline, and defends the plan with the cost figures. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as an event assistant (on-the-job training), a meeting and event planner (four-year study) and a catering manager (experience): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-hospitality-travel | Field task day. The student builds a three-day itinerary for an invented tour group within a budget and given schedules, plans for one access need, and defends the plan with the times and costs. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a tour guide (on-the-job training), a travel agent (certificate) and a recreation director (four-year study): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-public-service-government-services | Field task day at higher demand than grade 8. From an invented town's table of service requests (potholes, broken lights), the student ranks repairs within a set budget, writes the public notice, and defends the ranking with the figures. The student also maps the program of study for one job, such as a public works crew member (on-the-job training), a permit technician (two-year study) and a city planner (four-year or graduate study): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-public-service-emergency-response | Field task day. The student orders invented, non-graphic call cards by a given priority protocol, assigns units on a map from given response times, and defends the assignments with the times. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as an emergency dispatcher (on-the-job training and a state certificate), an emergency medical technician (certificate and a state license) and a firefighter (academy and certificates): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-public-service-judicial | Field task day. The student organizes an invented small claims case file (a dispute over a repair) into a timeline and an evidence list for the clerk, and defends the order with the documents. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a court clerk (on-the-job training), a paralegal (certificate or two-year study) and a judge or lawyer (four-year study, law school and a bar license): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-public-service-military | Field task day. The student compares three routes to one skilled job (aircraft mechanic, say): enlisting, a two-year program and an apprenticeship, by training time, pay while training, the service or work commitment (for enlisting: a contract of set years, a job that depends on the contract and the service's needs, and possible deployment; and the returns of pay, housing and education benefits) and the credential at the end, and whether it carries to civilian work (an aircraft mechanic still sits the federal exams), from dated public figures, and defends which fits a stated invented worker. Figures come from dated public federal pages, never recruiting material; no branch is named as a pick. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as an enlisted technician (enlistment and military training), a military officer (a four-year degree, through an academy, college officer training or officer school after college) and a civilian national security analyst (four-year study): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-public-service-public-safety | Field task day. From an invented, non-violent scene description (a lost bicycle, a fender bender), the student writes a factual incident report and reads dated staffing and training figures for the field, defending the report against a given report checklist. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a security officer (on-the-job training, and a state license where the state requires one), a police officer (academy and certification), a corrections officer (academy training) and a probation officer (four-year study and training): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-supply-chain-logistics | Field task day at higher demand than grade 8. The student plans a multi-stop route with delivery windows for invented stops, compares two plans by time and cost, and defends the chosen plan with the figures. The student also maps the program of study for one job, such as a dispatcher (on-the-job training), a logistics analyst (four-year study) and a logistics manager (experience): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-supply-chain-air-space | Field task day. The student builds an invented aircraft turnaround timeline (unloading, cleaning, fuel, loading) to meet a departure time and checks the bags against a weight limit, defending the timeline with the figures. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a ramp agent (on-the-job training), an aircraft mechanic (an approved school and federal certificates) and a pilot or air traffic controller (flight training or the federal academy): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-supply-chain-ground-rail | Field task day. The student fits an invented bus route timetable to the stops, run times and a given driver hours rule, and defends the timetable with the figures. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a bus driver (a commercial driver's license), a rail conductor (employer training) and a transportation planner (four-year study): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-supply-chain-maintenance-repair | Field task day. From an invented vehicle complaint and a symptom chart, the student orders the checks from least to most costly, estimates the repair from parts and labor hours, and defends the order with the figures. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a lube technician (on-the-job training), an automotive or diesel service technician (certificate or two-year study) and a service manager (experience): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-supply-chain-marine | Field task day. The student plans cargo loading on an invented small barge from container weights and a given balance rule (each side within a set difference), and defends the plan with the figures. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a deckhand (on-the-job training), a marine engineer (four-year maritime study) and a ship's captain (sea time and a federal license): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 
g10-sub-supply-chain-warehousing | Field task day. From an invented supply room's weekly use and delivery times, the student sets reorder points and order sizes, and defends them with the figures. The student maps the program of study for one job, such as a warehouse associate (on-the-job training), a purchasing agent (two-year or four-year study) and an inventory manager (experience): the high school courses, the training route and the first credential. 

