95:| 1 | Me, the plan and the tools. What do I need to know about myself and about a job to make a first choice worth making? Hook: three invented workers' days told in their own words. | 16 | g8-cd3-4-cluster-map; g8-prac-10-plan-path; g8-cd2-5-values; g8-cd1-2-first-plan; g8-cd1-3-goals; g8-all1-1-school-and-goals; g8-cd3-1-why-career-info; g8-cd3-2-job-study; g8-cd3-3-judge-sources; g8-prac-5-digital-data; g8-cd2-1-how-i-decide; g8-cd2-2-decision-steps; g8-cd2-3-facts-for-choices; g8-prac-12-academic-skills | CD3.K4; CD3.A4; CTE.Career Ready Practices.10; CD2.K5; CD1.K2; CD1.A2; CD1.K3; ALL1.K1; CD3.K1; CD3.A1; CD3.K2; CD3.A2; CD3.K3; CD3.A3; CTE.Career Ready Practices.5; CD2.K1; CD2.A1; CD2.K2; CD2.A2; CD2.K3; CD2.A3; CTE.Career Ready Practices.12 | 5 (digital tools and data), 10 (planning one's path), 12 (applying school skills) | In this order: an interest and strengths inventory used as reflection, never a sorting test, and a ranked card set of work values (practice 10's first half, CD2.K5); the cluster map of the 14 clusters, ways jobs are grouped, and three options in two clusters found from one interest (CD3.K4, CD3.A4); the parts of a model plan and the student's near and far goals (CD1.K2, CD1.K3); the first plan written, with two clusters linked from the inventory and the school subjects that lead toward one job (CD1.A2, practice 10, ALL1.K1); why a plan built on old facts misleads, and the kinds of career information and where each is found (CD3.K1, CD3.K2); a job study of one job on the job card, three kinds of information, each figure with its source and year, marked national (CD3.A2, two days); four sources on one job checked by lateral reading (CD3.K3, CD3.A3); job card figures sorted into a table and one decision it supports, each pair entering and sorting two rows at the shared device with one privacy step named, the rest on paper (practice 5, see Devices); the student's own way of choosing and the decision steps card run on a real choice in the course, which job the field task compares first (CD2.K1, CD2.A1, CD2.K2, CD2.A2); facts about self, job and training used to choose, and one plan line a job study changed (CD2.K3, CD2.A3, CD3.A1); the school skill a task used and the first portfolio choice with a reason (practice 12, on the plan day). Field task day: choose between two jobs with the decision steps card and the table, defended with one figure. Out: scored interest instruments (none is adapted); local labor data (a school adds it) | It introduces every model the cluster units use: the plan, the job card, the cluster map, the decision steps card, the data table and the portfolio (data/progressions/cte-edges.md: these rows come before any cluster unit), and the lens's order starts with the self before the world of work. Readiness items in day 1's last ten minutes: a percent of a total and a dated pay figure (math 7 retrieval, `m7-percent-problems`, `m7-unit-rate-fractions` for an hourly wage, `m7-compare-populations` for the table), and library `g6-lateral-reading` (grade 6). | Candidate: one unseen data item reading a dated hourly wage and its yearly figure; the decision steps card run on an invented student's choice. | No task asks about family income or work. The inventory is private and tells no student what job to do. Values cards are ranked privately on the plan page. The plan is private, scored on its reasoning, never on the path chosen. Tools: a dated job profile (one shared screen), a free offline spreadsheet on one shared device (practice 5's turn; nothing personal entered, the file kept on the school device) and a private plan template, each with a paper path; the inventory, the values cards, the cluster map, the job card, the decision steps card, the four-source set (an invented old article and an invented recruiting ad beside a dated handbook page) and the plan page are Curio Class's to write. Cost, class of 30, provisional (ours, 2026-11-25): printing about 300 pages, about $15 at $0.05 a page; no supplies. Sensitivity read: disabled workers among the hook's workers (the agent first studies disability-led organizations' published pages on work and the federal Office of Disability Employment Policy's pages). |
96:| 2 | The cross-cutting clusters. Every business needs people who use data, run the business and sell its work: which of those jobs could fit me? | 14 | g8-clu-digital-tech; g8-sub-digital-tech-data-science-ai; g8-clu-mgmt-entre; g8-sub-mgmt-entre-entrepreneurship; g8-clu-marketing-sales; g8-sub-marketing-sales-retail; g8-cd4-1-skill-kinds; g8-cd4-6-self-employment; g8-prac-4-teamwork; g8-prac-9-resources; g8-cd1-3r-revisit-goals | CTE.Career Clusters.Digital Technology; CTE.Career Clusters.Digital Technology.Data Science & Artificial Intelligence; CTE.Career Clusters.Management & Entrepreneurship; CTE.Career Clusters.Management & Entrepreneurship.Entrepreneurship & Small Business; CTE.Career Clusters.Marketing & Sales; CTE.Career Clusters.Marketing & Sales.Retail & Customer Experience; CD4.K1; CD4.K6; CTE.Career Ready Practices.4; CTE.Career Ready Practices.9; CD1.R3 | 4 (teamwork across differences), 9 (stewardship of money and resources); 5 and 10 return | Digital Technology (two explore days: its 6 sub-clusters placed on the map, job cards for a help desk technician, a web developer and a data analyst, compared on pay, training time, hours and conditions; the skills on the cards sorted into school, job-specific and general skills, CD4.K1), then its field task: one relationship found in a small table, a claim with its limits and one way an automated system could misread the data (Data Science and Artificial Intelligence). Management and Entrepreneurship (two explore days with three job cards), then its field task over two days: a one-day school stand planned in a mixed team with role cards, priced from an invented supply list within an invented budget, with the sales that cover the costs (Entrepreneurship and Small Business; practice 4's look-fors, practice 9's first half). One day on working for yourself and a wage against costs: two invented owners' accounts and the stand give the upsides and risks (CD4.K6), and a dated hourly wage is compared with invented weekly costs by percent and unit rate (practice 9's second half). Marketing and Sales (two explore days), then its field task: a complaint card answered in writing and a week's sales table read to decide a restock (Retail and Customer Experience). Plan day: goals reread and one changed with its cause (CD1.R3, the first of the course's plan-day rereads). Out: the other 13 sub-clusters at depth (Pathways); any live automated tool | The three cross-cutting clusters run through every other cluster (STANDARD.md section 1), so they come first and make later units' jobs easier to place. Needs unit 1's job card, cluster map and data table. Retrieval inside the field task days: `m7-percent-problems` (markup, a budget), `m7-unit-rate-fractions` (an hourly wage), `m7-compare-populations` (two weeks of sales), `g7-viz-choices` (a fair chart). | Candidate: one unseen item per cluster from its data sheet (a growth figure, a price that covers costs, a sales comparison). | Costs and prices are invented; no task touches a family's money. Regulation and the politics of business rules are shown as jobs and tasks, with no verdict on any policy or party. Automated tools described, not used, and no product named. Tools: a pay table reader (shared screen, paper path); Curio Class writes three data sheets, nine job cards, three field task sheets, the lunch-count table, the supply list, the complaint cards and the sales table, each invented set marked. Cost, class of 30, provisional: printing about 280 pages, about $14; role cards reused all year. Sensitivity read: disabled workers and one worker from a group seldom shown in each cluster (the agent studies the same disability sources as unit 1). |
97:| 3 | Making and building. Who makes, builds and moves the things we use, and how do they learn the work? | 15 | g8-clu-adv-manufacturing; g8-sub-adv-manufacturing-production; g8-clu-construction; g8-sub-construction-skilled-trades; g8-clu-supply-chain; g8-sub-supply-chain-logistics; g8-prac-1-contributor; g8-prac-3-problem-solving; g8-prac-7-time-space; g8-prac-11-effects; g8-cd4-3-general-skills; g8-cd4-4-carry-over-skills; g8-cd2-9-trade-offs; g8-all1-7-school-opens-options; g8-all2-5-routes-after-school; g8-cd2-4-options | CTE.Career Clusters.Advanced Manufacturing; CTE.Career Clusters.Advanced Manufacturing.Production & Automation; CTE.Career Clusters.Construction; CTE.Career Clusters.Construction.Skilled Trades; CTE.Career Clusters.Supply Chain & Transportation; CTE.Career Clusters.Supply Chain & Transportation.Planning & Logistics; CTE.Career Ready Practices.1; CTE.Career Ready Practices.3; CTE.Career Ready Practices.7; CTE.Career Ready Practices.11; CD4.K3; CD4.A3; CD4.K4; CD2.K9; CD2.A9; ALL1.K7; ALL2.K5; CD2.K4; CD2.A4 | 1 (professional contribution), 3 (critical thinking and persistence), 7 (managing time and space), 11 (effects of decisions); 4 returns | Advanced Manufacturing (two explore days), then a two-day field task: a paper production line planned on a timeline with a workstation layout (practice 7), run and timed, the slowest step found and a change proposed; the first fix fails by design, two fixes are weighed and a second try made (practice 3), and each student keeps a role and deadline and owns one slip in a note (practice 1); one hazard on a real production line and its control named as the worker would name it, from the young worker safety curriculum (Talking Safety), practiced, not owned; dependability and problem solving judged with two look-fors (CD4.A3) after two invented workplace cases on honesty and problem solving (CD4.K3). Construction (two explore days, apprenticeship as a route that pays while it trains), then its field task: tiles or boards estimated from a floor plan with a waste allowance (Skilled Trades). Supply Chain and Transportation (two explore days; three skills traced from one job card to a job in another cluster, CD4.K4), then its field task: a delivery route for invented stops compared by time and fuel, with who and what the route affects and one change for a better effect (Planning and Logistics; practice 11). Two days on routes after school: the seven routes (apprenticeship, certificate, two-year and four-year study, the military, online study, training on the job), each with one dated cost and time, none the default (ALL2.K5); the training required on three job cards and how school results keep each route open, unranked (ALL1.K7); what each of two job choices gives up, and two trade-offs the student might accept, each with a gain and a cost (CD2.K9, CD2.A9); four options and a likely result of each for an invented student's next step after high school, and one added option shown on the plan (CD2.K4, CD2.A4). Out: tool use beyond scissors; the full program of study (Pathways) | Making and building gives the first team field task with a planned timeline and a planned failure, so practices 1, 3 and 7 are taught on work that has a real deadline, and the trades and supply chain put paid-training routes early, which is where the routes day belongs, before the default four-year assumption sets in. Needs unit 1's decision steps card and job card, and unit 2's team roles. Retrieval: `m7-percent-problems` (waste allowance), `m7-unit-rate-fractions` (time and fuel per mile). Course-level check 1 follows. | Candidate: one unseen item per cluster (a timing table, a materials estimate, a route rate); one route-cost item read with its year. | Safety (days.csv words): "Scissors are counted out and back in; cut away from the body, seated, at the table; five minutes of clean-up close the production line days." Paper task only for Construction: no tools unless the school adds a lab with its own safety training. The military is one real route with its costs and returns (years of service committed), neither promoted nor condemned, no recruiter material on a student page; a teacher-page note says the school's own policy governs recruiter visits. Tools: an outlook and pay figure reader (shared screen, paper path); Curio Class writes three data sheets, nine job cards, three field task sheets, the paper product template, the floor plan, the route map with stops and rates, the two workplace cases and the routes sheet with dated costs. Cost, class of 30, provisional: printing about 320 pages, about $16; scrap paper and school scissors, $0 to $10. Sensitivity read: the military route (the agent first studies the federal defense department's own published service-commitment pages beside veterans' organizations' published guidance for schools, so the route is neither recruiting nor dismissal), disabled workers in the trades. |
98:| 4 | Land and energy. Every meal and every light switch depends on someone's work: what is that work, and how is it changing? | 11 | g8-clu-agriculture; g8-sub-agriculture-plant-systems; g8-clu-energy-resources; g8-sub-energy-resources-utilities; g8-cd5-1-society-needs; g8-cd5-2-economic-conditions; g8-cd5-3-hiring-trends; g8-cd1-5-plans-change; g8-cd4-5-moving-for-work; g8-all2-1-lifelong-learning; g8-prac-6-resilience | CTE.Career Clusters.Agriculture; CTE.Career Clusters.Agriculture.Plant Systems; CTE.Career Clusters.Energy & Natural Resources; CTE.Career Clusters.Energy & Natural Resources.Utilities; CD5.K1; CD5.K2; CD5.K3; CD1.K5; CD1.A5; CD4.K5; ALL2.K1; CTE.Career Ready Practices.6 | 6 (resilience); 5 and 11 return | Agriculture (two explore days; dated figures for one job in a city and a rural county and how moving, commuting or remote work change the chances, CD4.K5), then its field task: a planter bed or seed tray planned for a set space and harvest date from a dated growing guide (Plant Systems). Energy and Natural Resources (two explore days; how a line worker's tasks changed over twenty years from dated descriptions, ALL2.K1, and an invented worker whose plant automates part of the job, with the worker's next two steps and the skills that carry, practice 6), then its field task: a year of an invented school's power or water use read, the costliest month found and one change proposed with its likely saving (Utilities). One day on society and the economy: two needs in society and the jobs that grow from each (CD5.K1), and a dated table of hiring in two years for one field with the condition that likely moved it (CD5.K2). One day on trends: dated ten-year outlook figures for jobs in two clusters compared, a projection named as not a promise and a national figure as not local (CD5.K3), and how an outlook figure or a new interest could change a plan, shown on the student's own plan (CD1.K5, CD1.A5). Out: labor-market theory (grade 12 Economics); a verdict on any energy or resource dispute | It follows the mid-year check because it reads change over time (hiring tables, ten-year outlooks, tasks that changed), which needs the data table read through units 1 to 3. Land and energy work is where automation, place and public need show most plainly to a grade 8 student. Readiness items in the last ten minutes of day 47 (the check's respond day). Retrieval: `m7-percent-problems` (a saving as a percent), `m7-compare-populations` (two outlook displays). | Candidate: one unseen outlook item (projection, not promise; national, not local) and one usage-table item. | Safety (days.csv words): "Where the school adds a seed tray: a bagged commercial growing medium, not garden soil; an adult opens and moistens it first to keep down dust; gloves, and hand washing after soil and seeds; no eating in the area; the paper plan is the task." Environmental Protection is a needed field, taught like any other. Resource Extraction sits in a live public dispute: its jobs, training and conditions and the main views are shown, with no verdict, and a school may swap that job card for another Energy sub-cluster's. Students' own views on environment and resources are respected; no student is put down or graded for them; tasks judge reasoning and evidence. All movers are invented workers; no student is asked about their own or their family's moves. Rural and tribal students planned for: farm and land work shown as skilled work with real routes. Tools: an outlook figure reader (shared screen, paper path); Curio Class writes two data sheets, six job cards, two field task sheets, the growing guide extract with its source and year (a state extension service's planting calendar), the usage table, the hiring table and the worker case. Cost, class of 30, provisional: printing about 230 pages, about $12; optional seed trays, soil and seeds, about $25. Sensitivity read: Resource Extraction and the communities near it, and tribal land and agriculture work (the agent first studies tribal nations' own published workforce and land-management pages and a tribal colleges association's published pages), disabled workers on farms and utilities. |
99:| 5 | Money and creative work. How do people earn a living keeping a business's books, or making art for a client? | 10 | g8-clu-financial-services; g8-sub-financial-services-accounting; g8-clu-arts-design; g8-sub-arts-design-design-digital; g8-prac-8-creativity; g8-all1-2-improve-schoolwork; g8-all1-3-study-habits; g8-all1-4-what-helps-me-learn; g8-all1-6-attitude-habits; g8-all2-2-learner-identity | CTE.Career Clusters.Financial Services; CTE.Career Clusters.Financial Services.Accounting; CTE.Career Clusters.Arts, Entertainment, & Design; CTE.Career Clusters.Arts, Entertainment, & Design.Design & Digital Arts; CTE.Career Ready Practices.8; ALL1.K2; ALL1.A2; ALL1.K3; ALL1.A3; ALL1.K4; ALL1.A4; ALL1.K6; ALL1.A6; ALL2.K2 | 8 (creativity); 12 returns; 2 practiced, not judged (its look-fors are first taught in unit 7) | In this order: Financial Services' first explore day (the employer's side of money, and the hook); on unit day 2, one day on how I learn (below); Financial Services' second explore day, then its field task: an invented small business's month of receipts sorted into income and costs, totaled, and the month's profit or loss reported in a short note to the owner (Accounting). The day on how I learn: ways to improve school work (ALL1.K2), three study habits and one chosen to practice for two weeks, with a short note on what it changed at the plan day (ALL1.K3, ALL1.A3), the strategies and conditions that help the student learn (ALL1.K4), how attitude and habits affect results from invented cases (ALL1.K6), and a private reflection on a time learning something changed how they saw themselves (ALL2.K2). Arts, Entertainment, and Design (two explore days: art as paid work with a client, constraints and a deadline), then its field task: an invented client brief (a poster for a school event with a size, three colors and a deadline), several ideas listed, two combined, two sketches made and one defended against the brief (Design and Digital Arts; practice 8). Plan day: one strategy used to improve a piece, with the before and after shown (ALL1.A2, ALL1.A4), and one helpful habit shown over the unit on the portfolio note (ALL1.A6). Out: a student's or family's own money (grade 12 Economics); fixed learning-style labels | The two clusters share a short unit because each has one compact field task; the learning-habit rows sit here because the unit's two pieces (a report and a design) are the first where the student plans their own improvement before the plan day, and the two-week habit practice runs from unit day 2 (course day 60, school day 100) to the plan day (course day 66, school day 110), two school weeks. It sits before unit 6 so the pay-gap unit comes after five units of data reading. Retrieval: `m7-percent-problems` (a cost as a percent of income). | Candidate: one unseen income-and-cost item; one item judging a design against a brief's points. | Personal finance belongs to Economics: no task touches a student's or family's own money, and Financial Services is taught as a cluster of jobs like any other. No fixed learning-style label is taught or assigned; the lines teach strategies and conditions. Tools: none beyond paper (a free drawing tool optional on one shared screen); Curio Class writes two data sheets, six job cards, two field task sheets, the receipt set, the client brief and the habit-tracking note. Cost, class of 30, provisional: printing about 200 pages, about $10; colored pencils from school stock. Sensitivity read: family money (the agent checks every receipt and case is a business's, never a household's), disabled artists and bookkeepers at work. |
100:| 6 | Serving people. Who teaches us, cares for us, feeds us and runs our towns, and who gets to do those jobs? This is the pay-gap unit. | 16 | g8-clu-education; g8-sub-education-teaching; g8-clu-healthcare-human; g8-sub-healthcare-human-physical-health; g8-clu-hospitality; g8-sub-hospitality-culinary; g8-clu-public-service; g8-sub-public-service-government-services; g8-cd2-7-stereotypes; g8-cd3-5-open-jobs; g8-cd3-6-uncommon-jobs; g8-cd2-6-life-shapes-choices; g8-cd2-8-luck-timing; g8-cd1-4-steering-habits | CTE.Career Clusters.Education; CTE.Career Clusters.Education.Teaching, Training & Facilitation; CTE.Career Clusters.Healthcare & Human Services; CTE.Career Clusters.Healthcare & Human Services.Physical Health; CTE.Career Clusters.Hospitality, Events & Tourism; CTE.Career Clusters.Hospitality, Events & Tourism.Culinary & Food Services; CTE.Career Clusters.Public Service & Safety; CTE.Career Clusters.Public Service & Safety.Local, State & Federal Services; CD2.K7; CD3.K5; CD3.A5; CD3.K6; CD2.K6; CD2.K8; CD1.K4 | 4, 9 and 11 return; 2 practiced, not judged (unit 7) | Education (two explore days), then its field task: a five-minute lesson on a skill the student has, taught to two or three classmates with one check question, and the plan revised from what the check showed (Teaching, Training and Facilitation). Healthcare and Human Services (two explore days; on the second, jobs anyone could consider listed from the map, and one job the student had not considered and few people of a group they choose hold, studied through a real worker's public account, CD3.K5, CD3.A5), then its field task: invented patient cards routed to the right worker with the job cards, one routing explained by training and scope (Physical Health). One day on who holds which jobs: dated figures on who holds three jobs and real workers' accounts from groups seldom seen in them, disabled workers among them, and how a stereotype could hide a job (CD2.K7); the gains and hurdles of a job uncommon for a group, from dated figures with their source and real accounts (CD3.K6). Hospitality, Events and Tourism (two explore days; invented workers' accounts of how school, work and home shaped a decision, CD2.K6, and of a chance meeting or hiring wave, luck against preparation, CD2.K8), then its field task: a lunch menu for twenty guests at a set cost per plate from an invented price sheet, with one swap that keeps it under the limit (Culinary and Food Services). Public Service and Safety (two explore days; two invented workers' paths and the steering habits each used, with the line that shows it, CD1.K4), then its field task: one line of a town's dated public budget read, the jobs it pays for listed and one service change proposed with the figure (Local, State and Federal Services). Out: the sensitive sub-clusters at depth (named on the map and in job cards only); clinical tasks of any kind | Four clusters that serve people in person, with the course's most sensitive ground, placed late so the class has worked together for five units and reads dated figures well before it reads pay and job gaps. Public Service and Safety comes last in the unit so its budget task follows grade 8 social studies' public budget work (Cross-subject). Retrieval: `m7-unit-rate-fractions` (cost per plate). | Candidate: one unseen item per cluster (a routing by scope, a cost per plate, a budget line); one item reading a dated who-holds-the-job figure as a pattern, never a verdict on a person. | Every section carries health's steps for when a student tells you something on its teacher page (the pay-gap unit, STANDARD.md section 6). Pay and job gaps by sex and race are shown with a dated source, never as a verdict on a person; a student never states their own group in class; first-generation, rural and tribal students and students who care for family or already work are planned for. Every patient is invented; no student is asked about their own or a family member's health. The menu names no student's allergy or diet. No task asks about a record or anyone's legal status. Safety (days.csv words): "The menu task is paper only; any cooking a school adds names its knife and heat safety steps and follows the school's food rules." A school may swap a section on Behavioral and Mental Health, Emergency Response, Judicial Systems, Public Safety or the military for another job card of the same cluster. Tools: a dated job profile and a who-holds-the-job figure reader (shared screen, paper path); Curio Class writes four data sheets, twelve job cards, four field task sheets, the patient cards, the price sheet, the budget-line extract with its source and year (a town's adopted budget on its own public site), and the invented workers' accounts; real workers' public accounts are chosen with their source and date. The who-holds-the-job figures come from the federal labor statistics table of employment by occupation, sex, race and Hispanic or Latino ethnicity (https://www.bls.gov/cps/cpsaat11.htm); as of 2026-11-25 its 2025 annual averages are built from eleven months (October 2025 missing) and it gives sex as the percent who are women only, and the unit 6 data sheet says both. Cost, class of 30, provisional: printing about 330 pages, about $17. Sensitivity read: women and men in jobs seldom held by their sex, Black, Latino, Asian American, Native and other workers in fields where they are few, and disabled workers (the agent first studies the federal labor statistics bureau's own notes on its employment-by-demographic tables, the federal women's bureau's pages, and disability-led organizations' published employment pages); the health disclosure steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md); public safety and force (named on cards only). |
101:| 7 | The next step. What will I need for high school and for a first job, and what does my plan say now? | 10 | g8-cd1-1-plan-keeps-going; g8-all2-4-move-to-high-school; g8-all2-6-programs; g8-all1-5-school-plan; g8-all2-7-learning-outside-class; g8-all2-3-own-learning; g8-all1-8-finding-information; g8-cd4-2-job-search-steps; g8-prac-2-communicate | CD1.K1; CD1.A1; ALL2.K4; ALL2.A4; ALL2.K6; ALL1.K5; ALL1.A5; ALL2.K7; ALL2.K3; ALL2.A3; ALL1.K8; CD4.K2; CTE.Career Ready Practices.2 | 2 (clear communication); 10 and 12 return | The ten days, in order: (1) the hook, an invented worker's plan at 14 and at 30 and why it changed, and the two steps on the student's own plan that move toward a goal (CD1.K1, CD1.A1); (2) the move to high school: what it needs (ALL2.K4), programs that lead toward one job on the plan from the school's and state's own lists (ALL2.K6), and next year's course choices drafted from the school's own course guide (ALL2.A4), the course's most consequential real choice, revised on the plan day; (3) first packet day: a self-directed job study of one sub-cluster the student chooses from the 58 not studied at depth, planning the questions and sources themselves (ALL2.K3, ALL2.A3), with the search and source check used, named (ALL1.K8); (4) first skill day: the steps of a job search with a model of each and one resume line from a school activity or from learning outside class that builds work skills, with no option that assumes a cost (CD4.K2, ALL2.K7); (5) second packet day, with the check-in with each student; (6) second skill day: an email to an invented employer asking for an informational interview, revised for its reader, and one spoken interview answer after a model answer, with the teacher or a card as interviewer (practice 2, CD4.K2), and a first job's rights, practiced, not owned: a safe workplace, the hours and task limits for workers under 16 and under 18, and who to ask, from the federal young workers pages (https://www.osha.gov/young-workers, with the date read); (7) field task day: one task from the chosen sub-cluster on the course's task template, the plan's changed line, and a short school plan for next term drafted as a section of the career plan (ALL1.K5, ALL1.A5), so it gets the plan day's feedback; (8 to 10) the plan day, the assessment day and the respond day. Out: the full resume and mock interview (Pathways, g10-cd4-resume-interview) | The year ends where the plan meets the next real choice (high school course selection, usually in the spring of grade 8), and the job search comes last because it uses every earlier unit's skills. The packet days need the whole year's job cards and the cluster map. Retrieval: none from math. ELA's presenting work runs alongside (Cross-subject). Course-level check 2 follows. | Candidate: one unseen item on a job search step; one email line revised for a reader. | Interview and resume tasks: no question an employer may not lawfully ask, nothing private, and a school address or none on the resume line. The teacher page states that an employer may not ask about a disability before a job offer, that a worker may ask for a reasonable change to how a job is done, and that no student discloses a disability in any course task. Packet choices follow the school's sensitive sub-cluster choices with a same-cluster alternative; a packet on a sensitive sub-cluster carries its teacher note and sensitivity read, and health's disclosure steps where STANDARD.md section 6 names its topic (Packet days, above). Course choices come from the school's own guide; a school adds its counselor and its state's required plan (STANDARD.md section 4). Tools: a mock-interview question bank with a model answer and a feedback list, and a private plan template (each with a paper path); Curio Class writes the packet's task template and check-in line (one per cluster, from the 14 data sheets), the model email and model answers, the invented worker's two plans and the job search step cards. Cost, class of 30, provisional: printing about 250 pages, about $13. Sensitivity read: students who already work or care for family, and first-generation students (the agent checks no task assumes a parent who knows the high school system), disabled job seekers and workplace rights stated plainly (the agent studies the federal Office of Disability Employment Policy's pages), and any packet on a sensitive sub-cluster (the agent reads it with that sub-cluster's sources before use). |
103:## Course-level checks
